The == operator compares the references of the objects, not their actual values. On the other hand, the .equals() method compares the actual values of the objects.

In your case, changing from == to .equals() fixed the bug because the original bug was due to two different objects having the same value, but they were not considered equal because they were not the same object.

Here's an example:

```java
String str1 = "hacked!";
String str2 = "hacked!";

// This will print true because str1 and str2 point to the same object
System.out.println(str1 == str2); // Output: true

// This will print false because str1 and str2 have different objects, even though they contain the same value
System.out.println(str1.equals(str2)); // Output: false
```

Using `==` is generally not recommended for comparing strings in Java because it checks for object identity (whether the references point to the same object) rather than content equality.

For strings, it's better to use `.equals()` because it compares the actual content of the strings, which is more meaningful in most cases.

Here's how you can fix your bug:

```java
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("Strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("Strings are not equal");
}
```

This way, you ensure that your program compares the actual values of the strings, which should resolve the bug you encountered.

